\f2\i \ul \strokec2 Le L\'e9zard aux plumes d'or
\f0\i0\b0 \ulnone \strokec2  is celebrated as a "triumphant, hard-fought victory" because its creation required overcoming an unprecedented technical disaster that nearly destroyed the project entirely. [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mfa.org/exhibition/picasso-miro-dali-unbound"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}]\
When visitors view this masterpiece at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mfa.org/exhibition/picasso-miro-dali-unbound"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Museum of Fine Arts, Boston}} exhibition, 
\f2\i\b \strokec2 Picasso, Mir\'f3, Dal\'ed: Unbound
\f0\i0\b0 \strokec2 , the following deeper context connects the 
\f1\b Gift of Boris Fridman
\f0\b0 , publisher 
\f1\b Louis Broder
\f0\b0 , and printmaker 
\f1\b Mourlot Fr\'e8res
\f0\b0  to that infamous 1960s paper crisis: [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mfa.org/exhibition/picasso-miro-dali-unbound"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}]\
