\f1\b\fs40 The Secret Disaster: The Scrapped 1967 Version\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Original Effort:
\f0\b0  Joan Mir\'f3 actually completed a full set of stunning color lithographs for 
\f3\i Le L\'e9zard
\f0\i0  in November 1967.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Chemical Flaw:
\f0\b0  After the entire first run was printed by Mourlot Fr\'e8res, publisher Louis Broder noticed a catastrophic defect. The paper chosen for the edition\'97a prestigious handmade parchment\'97reacted poorly with the unique chemical composition of Mourlot's heavy lithographic inks. [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.schloss-ahlden.de/assets/images/auctions/catalogue-139.pdf"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mfa.org/exhibition/picasso-miro-dali-unbound"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 2}}]\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Degradation:
\f0\b0  Over time, the ink began to bleed unevenly, altering Mir\'f3's exacting vision of sharp lines and vibrant, un-muddled color fields. [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mfa.org/exhibition/picasso-miro-dali-unbound"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}]\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Total Scrap:
\f0\b0  Refusing to compromise on quality, Broder and Mir\'f3 made the agonizing decision to reject and physically destroy the entire completed 1967 edition, rendering years of painstaking work completely obsolete.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 \

\f1\b\fs40 The 1971 Triumph: Mourlot and Broder's Redemption\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Starting Over:
\f0\b0  To salvage the project, Mir\'f3 had to completely recreate his vision on entirely new lithographic stones and plates.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Flawless Paper Chemistry:
\f0\b0  Mourlot Fr\'e8res spent years reformulating their technical approach, eventually settling on flawlessly manufactured, watermarked 
\f1\b B.F.K. Rives paper
\f0\b0  (and a highly limited run on pristine vellum/parchment) that could permanently hold the saturation of Mir\'f3\'92s primary colors without chemical degradation.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Final Masterpiece:
\f0\b0  Released finally in 1971, the book emerged not just as an art piece, but as a legendary symbol of printmaking resilience. [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.schloss-ahlden.de/assets/images/auctions/catalogue-139.pdf"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mfa.org/exhibition/picasso-miro-dali-unbound"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 2}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.google.com/search?q=product&prds=pvt:hg,productid:12440717705667319651&ibp=oshop"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 3}}]\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 \

\f1\b\fs40 Connecting the Gift: The Vision of Boris Fridman\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Preserving the Struggle:
\f0\b0  This specific physical history is what makes the copy on display\'97the 
\f1\b Gift of Boris Fridman
\f0\b0 \'97so vital to the MFA's collection.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Collector's Discerning Eye:
\f0\b0  As a collector, Boris Fridman did not just seek out beautiful books; he specifically collected 
\f3\i livres d\'92artiste
\f0\i0  (artists' books) that represented the pinnacle of collaborative tension and mechanical triumph.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Why it Matters to "Unbound":
\f0\b0  By donating this hard-fought 1971 victory to the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, Fridman allowed the museum to showcase the absolute limit of 20th-century printmaking. It proves to visitors that an "unbound" artist's book is a living, breathing laboratory where artists, publishers, and chemists fought tooth and nail to push art forward. [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mfa.org/exhibition/picasso-miro-dali-unbound"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9BHQcD7dZPs"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 3}}]\
